# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_COUNTER_CREATE */
int64_t counter_create(uint64_t options, uint32_t *out);
/* CROI_SYS_COUNTER_ADD */
int64_t counter_add(croi_counter_add_item_t *items, uint64_t count);
/* CROI_SYS_COUNTER_READ */
int64_t counter_read(uint32_t counter, int64_t *value);

int64_t croi_counter_add(uint32_t counter, int64_t amount);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A counter holds a signed 64-bit value, 0 when created.
It asserts `CROI_COUNTER_POSITIVE` while the value is above 0 and `CROI_COUNTER_NON_POSITIVE` while it is not.
It is the one event object: the doorbell that wakes the other side of a ring, the fence a frame waits on.
A program waits on one with object_bind(2): `CROI_BIND_VALUE` queues a packet once the counter reaches a value, `CROI_BIND_CHANGE` one at every add, and `CROI_BIND_SIGNALS` on the two signals above.
A counter given a deadline profile by object_set_profile(2) lends it to each thread that its packets wake.

`counter_create` writes a handle to a new counter to <out>.
<options> must be 0.
The handle has the basic rights, READ, WRITE and SIGNAL.

`counter_add` adds <amount> to the counter of each of up to 64 items:

```c
typedef struct {
  uint32_t counter;  // the counter's handle
  int32_t status;    // out: 0, or why this item failed
  int64_t amount;
} croi_counter_add_item_t;
```

Each counter handle needs READ and WRITE (the result reveals the value).
Items are all done: a failed one, such as a counter that would overflow, which is `CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE` and unchanged, does not stop the later ones; a service ringing every client's doorbell rings the rest when one client has gone.
The call returns how many succeeded and writes each item's <status>.
`croi_counter_add` adds to one counter and returns 0, the item's status or the call's.

`counter_read` writes the value to <value>, which must not be null.
The handle needs READ.

